<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Brand color or world color: a field study of Czech TV advertising</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<style>{{ inline_css }}</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<header class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">A field study, 2026</p>
    <h1>Brand color, or world color?</h1>
    <p class="lede">A spot's picture either commits to the brand color or commits to a world. Of {{ counts.total }} Czech TV spots from {{ counts.n_brands }} brands, {{ counts.close }} flood with brand color, {{ counts.far }} cede the picture to a world, and {{ counts.mid }} sit in between. Few realize they are choosing.</p>
    <p class="byline">By {{ author }}. <a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a>.</p>
</header>

<section class="method">
    <p class="method-label">Method</p>
    <p class="method-body">{{ counts.total }} publicly available Czech TV spots across {{ counts.n_brands }} brands, sampled at one frame per second. Per-frame palettes via K-means (k = 5) in canonical CIE-LAB. For each spot we compute its dominant chromatic hue, the brand's anchor hue, and the circular distance between them. Strip and radial views aggregate frames across all spots of a brand; the bar chart in section 01 shows every spot separately.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">01</span><h2>The split</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Distance, in degrees on the hue wheel, between each brand's anchor swatch and the dominant chromatic color the spot actually puts on screen. One bar per spot ({{ counts.total }} spots from {{ counts.n_brands }} brands), bars filled with the swatch; the dashed reference at 90&deg; marks the perceptual threshold beyond which the two are, effectively, different colors. {{ counts.close }} spots sit below 20&deg;. {{ counts.far }} sit above 100&deg;. {{ counts.mid }} sit in between.</p>
    <figure class="gap-frame">
        {{ gap_svg | safe }}
    </figure>
</section>

<aside class="pull">
    <p class="pull-q">AAA Auto's brand yellow occupies <span class="num">0.001%</span> of screen-time.</p>
    <p class="pull-cap">A logo carries the brand identity. The picture commits to a different world: dim grays, neutral browns, the visual register of a used-car catalog. Both signals can coexist; rarely is the split deliberate.</p>
</aside>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">02</span><h2>The two camps, on screen</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Each spot reads as a contact sheet: every frame a vertical column, every column split top to bottom by K-means cluster sizes. Strips are ordered by their distance from brand color, so the page reads brand-led at the top, world-led at the bottom. A saturation sparkline sits below each brand name; the arc glyph summarizes its shape.</p>
    <div class="strips-list">
{% for row in brand_rows %}
    <div class="brand-row">
        <div class="brand-meta">
            <p class="name"><span class="swatch" style="background: {{ row.anchor_hex }};"></span>{{ row.brand }}</p>
            <p class="sector">{{ row.sector }} · {{ row.n_frames }} frames · arc {{ row.arc_glyph }} {{ row.arc_shape }}</p>
            <div class="brand-spark">{{ row.sparkline_svg | safe }}</div>
        </div>
        {{ row.strip_html | safe }}
    </div>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">03</span><h2>Where each spot's color mass lives</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Per-spot polar histograms. Angle is hue (0&deg; red, 120&deg; green, 240&deg; blue); radial length is saturation-weighted density. The tick on the rim, in anchor color, marks each brand's logo swatch. In two cases the tick aligns with the spot's color mass. In the rest, the tick is somewhere the picture barely visits.</p>
    <div class="radial-grid">
{% for row in brand_rows %}
    <figure class="radial-cell">
        {{ row.radial_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption><span class="rc-name">{{ row.brand }}</span><span class="rc-sector">{{ row.sector }}</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">04</span><h2>The split is structural, not sectoral</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Hierarchical clustering on pairwise Sz&eacute;kely energy distance between every pair of brands' weighted LAB palettes. If the brand-vs-world split were just a sector artifact, brands from the same sector would cluster tightly. They do not. McDonald's two spots sit close to each other; everyone else clusters across sector boundaries, by their color signature.</p>
    <figure class="dendrogram-frame">
        {{ dendrogram_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption class="scatter-cap">Energy distance is a kernel-based statistical distance on weighted point clouds; lower means more similar full-palette distributions.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">05</span><h2>The category effect, supporting</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Saturation tension (the std of per-frame saturation across the spot) splits the corpus by category too. Banking and automotive flatten; performative formats swing. This is a related but distinct axis from the brand-vs-world split; both shape the spot.</p>
    <figure class="scatter-frame">
        {{ scatter_svg | safe }}
        <figcaption class="scatter-cap">Palette diversity (x) vs saturation std across frames (y). Quadrants at corpus medians.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="sector-ci-block">
        <p class="ci-eyebrow">Sector means with 95% bootstrap CI on saturation std</p>
        {{ sector_ci_svg | safe }}
        <p class="ci-note">Bootstrap resamples brand-level values within each sector. With two to three brands per sector the intervals are wide; the gap between corporate (banking, automotive) and performative sectors is still visible.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">06</span><h2>Patterns</h2></div>
    <div class="patterns">
{% for p in patterns %}
    <article class="pattern">
        <p class="num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
        <div>
            <h3>{{ p.title }}</h3>
            <p class="evidence">{{ p.evidence }}</p>
            <p class="takeaway"><strong>Why it matters</strong>{{ p.takeaway }}</p>
        </div>
    </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">07</span><h2>Playbook</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Five moves a strategist or creative director can run on next week's pitch.</p>
    <div class="playbook">
{% for move in playbook %}
    <article class="play">
        <p class="play-num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
        <div>
            <h3>{{ move.title }}</h3>
            <p class="play-body">{{ move.body }}</p>
        </div>
    </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<footer>
    <div>
        <h4>Colophon</h4>
        <p>Set in Bodoni Moda and Manrope.</p>
        <p>Single-file HTML; no JavaScript.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Code</h4>
        <p><a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a></p>
        <p>MIT for code, CC&#8209;BY&#8209;4.0 for derived data.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Disclaimer</h4>
        <p>Analysis of publicly available video material. Brands and campaigns are referenced for analytical purposes only. No agency attribution is implied.</p>
    </div>
</footer>

</main>
</body>
</html>
